{"id": "biz-5", "headline": "Rising costs push apple growers to tear out orchards", "source": "NPR", "url": "https://www.npr.org/sections/the-picture-show/2026/11/24/g-s1-154620/how-high-costs-are-squeezing-apple-producers", "published": "2026-11-24", "url_verified": true, "pages": ["In Washington state, the nation's biggest apple region, growers are tearing out orchards that no longer pay. Farmer Sean Gilbert, near Wapato, said it costs him less to leave ground fallow than to replant. Washington's $9 billion industry grows nearly 70% of the country's fresh apples, according to Jon DeVaney of the Washington State Tree Fruit Association. He said farmers face higher costs for labor, fuel, fertilizer and equipment, while tariffs and a strong dollar weaken exports. DeVaney estimated thousands of acres of orchards across the Northwest are being removed or left unplanted. The squeeze could mean a smaller domestic apple supply in coming years. Source: NPR."], "qr": {"n": 41, "rows": ["11111110101001101100110000101110001111111", "10000010010001101011110000001111101000001", "10111010011001100001011001001010001011101", "10111010001110101010110001001100001011101", "10111010001111011100010001101111001011101", "10000010010110111011011111100000101000001", "11111110101010101010101010101010101111111", "00000000100011011010000011000001100000000", "11011010000000101110100000100111101000001", "00110100111001010101111100010011010111100", "11001011110101011000101111010100001000100", "11010000000001010011000100110101111011001", "00101010100101000101100100101010101100010", "01111000101011100011100101111110001010011", "01000110010000010101010001111101101010101", "01001000100010001010100000111000010001101", "11100011010101110010000001000001110001000", "01111000000010111111001001010001100010000", "00101010011111111111010101010101111001101", "00000100101101001000000111011000011110111", "00111111000011011010000010100100001000100", "11100101101000000100110111110001100111110", "10101010011010100